  "prompt": "Create ONE NEW independent native transparent NW running sprite of the CALLIGRAPHER BOY. Image1 (the boy) supplies identity, anatomy, outfit, head size and rear-left three-quarter NW camera, NOT a static pose to retain. Image2 (the archer girl) supplies the actual RUNNING BODY POSE: reproduce its anatomical near/far thigh overlap, flexion of each knee and ankle, forefoot/heel contact versus flight, and counter-swing rhythm using the BOY'S short proportions. Do not reuse a previous wrong pose just because it was in image1. Image3 is approved painterly/material style only. NW travel points diagonally away toward upper-left. Both boots' long axes follow upper-left NW with continuous knees/ankles, no external toe yaw and no mirrored flip. Draw distinct left and right legs from their actual hip joints following image2; baggy black short pants remain, not bare girl's legs. Preserve boy identity: brown compact topknot and taiji hairpiece, teal ribbons, charcoal black/gold cloud-pattern robe with ivory lining, teal belt and ORIGINAL decorations, gold-trim black boots. Anatomical LEFT arm holds a blank paper scroll with exactly TWO tiny pale-cyan ink spirits; anatomical RIGHT arm grips a large black/gold calligraphy brush, one teal tassel visibly chained to its gold BUTT end. Keep hands connected through the correct left/right shoulder and elbow, not merely screen-side prop swapping. Allow naturally bent elbows and forward/back arm motion; no stiff horizontal T arms. Never copy archer's face, long hair, green/white outfit, skirt, bow, quiver, arrows or bare legs. Single whole boy framed like 17 NW sprites, natural small body bounce, consistent head size/camera, no arbitrary image translation or ground snapping. Full square transparent RGBA canvas natively at least1024; aim1254. No text, no drawn ground/outline markers, no shadow disc, no frame sheet.\nREQUIRED PHASE: OPPOSITE EARLY LOADING after FAR RIGHT foot contact. Far RIGHT shin and boot lead upper-left under body; NEAR LEFT knee folded back, calf/sole lifted to lower-right in front of far leg. Near left thigh crosses visually in front of the far right thigh. This must be the opposite anatomical support of02, not another left stance. Left scroll arm forward, right brush arm back.",
